1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:03,029
The Exchange Banks of Raffles Place:

2
00:00:03,109 --> 00:00:05,700
Chartered, HSBC and Mercantile

3
00:00:05,700 --> 00:00:09,205
For a century, the money
that moved Singapore's trade

4
00:00:09,285 --> 00:00:12,600
passed through a few
buildings around one square.

5
00:00:12,600 --> 00:00:16,441
The Chartered Bank, the Hongkong
and Shanghai Bank and the

6
00:00:16,521 --> 00:00:20,429
Mercantile Bank of India were
British "exchange banks," set

7
00:00:20,509 --> 00:00:24,282
up to finance trade between
Asia and Europe, and from the

8
00:00:24,362 --> 00:00:28,350
1850s they made Raffles Place
the colony's banking quarter.

9
00:00:28,350 --> 00:00:32,547
Their domed and columned offices
were replaced, one after another,

10
00:00:32,627 --> 00:00:35,916
by the towers that stand
there now, and two of those

11
00:00:35,996 --> 00:00:39,625
towers were built by the same
banks on the same corners.

12
00:00:39,625 --> 00:00:44,047
Singapore's first banks, which I
wrote about in an earlier post,

13
00:00:44,127 --> 00:00:46,800
were branches of banks based in India.

14
00:00:46,800 --> 00:00:49,791
The exchange banks that
followed were British

15
00:00:49,871 --> 00:00:52,600
banks chartered to work across the East.

16
00:00:52,600 --> 00:00:56,878
Their main business was trade
finance: a merchant shipping rubber,

17
00:00:56,958 --> 00:00:59,849
tin or pepper to London
could sell the bank a

18
00:00:59,929 --> 00:01:04,670
bill of exchange, a written order for
payment due when the goods arrived,

19
00:01:04,750 --> 00:01:07,325
and be paid in Singapore straight away.

20
00:01:07,325 --> 00:01:11,789
Because the bills were paid in
pounds in London and in dollars here,

21
00:01:11,869 --> 00:01:14,862
the banks dealt in foreign
exchange every day,

22
00:01:14,942 --> 00:01:18,350
and their rates set the
price of money in the port.

23
00:01:18,350 --> 00:01:23,057
Until the government began issuing
its own currency notes in 1899,

24
00:01:23,137 --> 00:01:28,641
the banks also issued banknotes, the
Chartered and the Mercantile among them,

25
00:01:28,721 --> 00:01:32,275
as I described in the post
on the Straits dollar.

26
00:01:32,275 --> 00:01:36,051
When the Chartered Bank laid
the foundation stone of a

27
00:01:36,131 --> 00:01:39,765
new building in 1953, the
Governor, Sir John Nicoll,

28
00:01:39,845 --> 00:01:43,335
credited the exchange banks
with the underwriting,

29
00:01:43,415 --> 00:01:47,700
issue and service of government
and municipal loans as well.

30
00:01:47,700 --> 00:01:51,658
The Mercantile Bank was
founded in Bombay in 1853,

31
00:01:51,738 --> 00:01:56,908
received a royal charter in 1857
as the Mercantile Bank of India,

32
00:01:56,988 --> 00:02:01,350
London and China, and moved
its head office to London.

33
00:02:01,350 --> 00:02:05,250
It came to Singapore in the mid-1850s.

34
00:02:05,250 --> 00:02:10,095
In 1961 the Straits Times
noted that the bank and its

35
00:02:10,175 --> 00:02:15,100
predecessors had operated
in Singapore for 105 years.

36
00:02:15,100 --> 00:02:20,500
By the late 1920s it had its
own building on Raffles Place.

37
00:02:20,500 --> 00:02:24,486
The Chartered Bank of
India, Australia and China

38
00:02:24,566 --> 00:02:29,225
received its royal charter
from Queen Victoria in 1853.

39
00:02:29,225 --> 00:02:32,806
It opened its first
Singapore branch in leased

40
00:02:32,886 --> 00:02:37,025
premises on the square on
the 19th of February 1859.

41
00:02:37,025 --> 00:02:41,190
The square had been Commercial
Square until the year before,

42
00:02:41,270 --> 00:02:43,675
when it was renamed Raffles Place.

43
00:02:43,675 --> 00:02:47,523
In 1895 the bank moved
into a building of its own,

44
00:02:47,603 --> 00:02:53,650
a three-storey palazzo at the corner of
Battery Road and Flint Street designed

45
00:02:53,730 --> 00:02:58,600
by the architects Swan and
Maclaren, which cost about $60,000.

46
00:02:58,600 --> 00:03:02,250
Within twenty years it
had outgrown that too.

47
00:03:02,250 --> 00:03:06,779
In 1916 it moved to new premises
at the corner of Battery Road

48
00:03:06,859 --> 00:03:11,462
and Bonham Street, facing Raffles
Place, a four-storey building

49
00:03:11,542 --> 00:03:16,225
with a dome that also housed the
Singapore Chamber of Commerce.

50
00:03:16,225 --> 00:03:19,425
The old palazzo was let to other tenants

51
00:03:19,505 --> 00:03:22,375
and later became the Bank of China.

52
00:03:22,375 --> 00:03:27,355
The Hongkong and Shanghai Bank
was founded in Hong Kong in 1865,

53
00:03:27,435 --> 00:03:32,575
and it did business in Singapore
through an agent from that year.

54
00:03:32,575 --> 00:03:35,219
It opened its own office on

55
00:03:35,299 --> 00:03:38,325
Collyer Quay in December 1877.

56
00:03:38,325 --> 00:03:42,005
A hundred years later, in
1977, the Straits Times

57
00:03:42,085 --> 00:03:46,226
reported that the bank was
celebrating the centenary of

58
00:03:46,306 --> 00:03:50,450
its "special agency," which
had in fact been a branch.

59
00:03:50,450 --> 00:03:54,253
In 1890 the bank bought the
corner site where Battery

60
00:03:54,333 --> 00:03:58,208
Road meets Collyer Quay, and
in October 1894 it opened

61
00:03:58,288 --> 00:04:02,384
a three-storey building there,
also by Swan and Maclaren,

62
00:04:02,464 --> 00:04:06,200
in a mix of Gothic,
Renaissance and Baroque styles.

63
00:04:06,200 --> 00:04:10,184
It was pulled down in
1919, and its replacement,

64
00:04:10,264 --> 00:04:15,175
the Hongkong Bank Chambers,
was finished in the mid-1920s.

65
00:04:15,175 --> 00:04:20,100
The early 20th century was the
exchange banks' busiest time.

66
00:04:20,100 --> 00:04:22,825
Rubber and tin from Malaya passed through

67
00:04:22,905 --> 00:04:26,325
Singapore to the world,
and the banks financed it.

68
00:04:26,325 --> 00:04:30,252
Raffles Place filled with
motor cars, and other banks

69
00:04:30,332 --> 00:04:34,561
arrived around the square,
among them the Bank of Taiwan,

70
00:04:34,641 --> 00:04:38,950
which took over the Chartered
Bank's old palazzo in 1916.

71
00:04:38,950 --> 00:04:41,775
The war ended that world.

72
00:04:41,775 --> 00:04:45,932
Raffles Place was hit in
the first Japanese air

73
00:04:46,012 --> 00:04:50,250
raid on Singapore, on
the 8th of December 1941.

74
00:04:50,250 --> 00:04:53,646
After the British
surrender in February 1942,

75
00:04:53,726 --> 00:04:57,975
the British banks were closed
and their staff interned.

76
00:04:57,975 --> 00:05:03,330
The Yokohama Specie Bank set up in the
Hongkong and Shanghai Bank's building,

77
00:05:03,410 --> 00:05:06,154
and later in the occupation the Japanese

78
00:05:06,234 --> 00:05:10,248
administration paid out part
of the deposits of the "enemy

79
00:05:10,328 --> 00:05:14,775
banks," as I described in the
post on the Yokohama Specie Bank.

80
00:05:14,775 --> 00:05:17,743
The British banks reopened after

81
00:05:17,823 --> 00:05:20,775
the Japanese surrender in 1945.

82
00:05:20,775 --> 00:05:24,750
The banks began to rebuild in the 1950s.

83
00:05:24,750 --> 00:05:28,723
On the 31st of July 1953 the
Governor laid the foundation

84
00:05:28,803 --> 00:05:32,990
stone of a new seven-storey
building for the Chartered Bank,

85
00:05:33,070 --> 00:05:36,688
which he called "as clear
an indication as there can

86
00:05:36,768 --> 00:05:40,750
be of the Bank's confidence
in the future of Singapore."

87
00:05:40,750 --> 00:05:45,708
Into a cavity beneath the stone
went newly minted Coronation coins,

88
00:05:45,788 --> 00:05:50,300
the previous day's newspapers
and the bank's annual reports.

89
00:05:50,300 --> 00:05:55,319
In 1959 the Hongkong and Shanghai
Bank bought the Mercantile Bank,

90
00:05:55,399 --> 00:05:59,182
which kept its own name
for many years afterwards,

91
00:05:59,262 --> 00:06:03,125
and in 1984 the Hongkong
bank sold it to Citibank.

92
00:06:03,125 --> 00:06:07,400
The Mercantile's name is no
longer seen in Singapore.

93
00:06:07,400 --> 00:06:10,921
The Chartered Bank
merged with the Standard

94
00:06:11,001 --> 00:06:14,350
Bank in 1969 to form Standard Chartered.

95
00:06:14,350 --> 00:06:18,181
In 1975 the Hongkong Bank
Chambers were pulled down,

96
00:06:18,261 --> 00:06:23,150
and the bank moved for a while
into the Ocean Building next door.

97
00:06:23,150 --> 00:06:26,865
Its new tower at the
corner of Collyer Quay

98
00:06:26,945 --> 00:06:30,475
and Battery Road opened in January 1983.

99
00:06:30,475 --> 00:06:34,952
The Standard Chartered
Building, a 44-storey tower at

100
00:06:35,032 --> 00:06:40,541
Six Battery Road, on the corner
the bank had occupied since 1916,

101
00:06:40,621 --> 00:06:43,975
was opened on the 24th of October 1984.

102
00:06:43,975 --> 00:06:48,430
From 1987 to 1997 I worked
for Security Pacific,

103
00:06:48,510 --> 00:06:54,650
an American bank whose Singapore
office was in the HSBC Building.

104
00:06:54,650 --> 00:06:57,902
For ten years I went to
work on the corner where

105
00:06:57,982 --> 00:07:01,860
the Hongkong and Shanghai Bank
had stood since the 1890s,

106
00:07:01,940 --> 00:07:04,925
without knowing how
long it had been there.

107
00:07:04,925 --> 00:07:08,948
Both banks have since moved
their main Singapore offices

108
00:07:09,028 --> 00:07:12,757
to Marina Bay, but the HSBC
Building and Six Battery

109
00:07:12,837 --> 00:07:16,500
Road still stand where
their first buildings rose.

110
00:07:16,500 --> 00:07:18,635
Where it fits in the bigger story:

111
00:07:18,715 --> 00:07:22,999
The exchange banks were the plumbing
of colonial Singapore's trade,

112
00:07:23,079 --> 00:07:26,075
and Raffles Place was
where that plumbing met.

113
00:07:26,075 --> 00:07:29,147
The square's buildings
changed several times,

114
00:07:29,227 --> 00:07:34,550
from palazzos to domes to towers, but
the banks' addresses changed far less.

115
00:07:34,550 --> 00:07:38,896
The towers that two of them built
still stand on the corners they

116
00:07:38,976 --> 00:07:42,844
took in the 19th and early
20th centuries, which makes the

117
00:07:42,924 --> 00:07:47,350
oldest part of the financial
district one of its most continuous.
